Here are some notes about adding to and using this testsuite.

The testsuite adds a directory containing the just-built cygwin1.dll to the PATH
so that it can be found by the Windows loader during testing.

Because we'll probably run into complaints about using two DLLs, we use
cygrun.exe to run each test.  All this does is run the test with CreateProcess()
so that we don't attempt to do the special code for when a cygwin program calls
another cygwin program, as this might be a "multiple cygwins" problem.

The testsuite/winsup.api subdirectory is for testing the API to cygwin1.dll
ONLY.  Create other subdirs under testsuite/ for other classes of testing.

Tests in testsuite/winsup.api/pthread/ are derived from the pthread-win32
testsuite.

Tests in testsuite/winsup.api/ltp/ are derived from (a very old version of) the
ltp testsuite.

Don't print anything to the screen if you can avoid it (except for failure
reasons, of course).

"make check" will only work if you run it *on* an NT machine.  Cross-checking is
not supported.

Host-side build-script tests in build/ can also be run directly without a built
DLL or cross compiler, for example:

$ bash winsup/testsuite/build/gentls_offsets.sh

In a configured cross build, the same host-only test can be run through the
Automake driver without building Windows test executables or invoking the
Windows-only check-local setup:

$ make -C BUILD/winsup/testsuite check-TESTS TESTS=build/gentls_offsets.sh check_PROGRAMS=

This exercises gentls_offsets with controlled preprocessor and assembly output,
including x86_64 .long, AArch64 .word, and compiler/parser failures.  It is not
a substitute for compiling cygtls.h with the target compiler or running the DLL.
An optional argument selects another copy of the generator for comparison.

The preprocessing fixture also requires cygtls.h to be included from a C++
translation unit rather than compiled as a main header, keeping pragma-once
diagnostics from breaking target generation with warnings treated as errors.

winsup.api/setjmp covers zero, positive and negative longjmp/siglongjmp return
values, both sigsetjmp mask modes, and AArch64 x20 preservation.
sigjmp-mask checks public-buffer guard bytes, the macro save-mask slot, and
repeated protected-page faults recovered through both the macro and function
sigsetjmp/siglongjmp interfaces.  A compiler/header/runtime cohort must agree
on these buffer layouts; an ARM64 Cygwin jmp_buf has a 256-byte register area.
ARM64 consumers built with the former 176-byte definition must be recompiled:
replacing only their DLL cannot enlarge embedded buffers or move macro mask
slots.  Rebuild runtime/newlib as a coherent cohort as well, because fork
startup records embed jmp_buf.  Preserve old cohort evidence separately.
ucontext-arm64 checks aligned context entry, register and stack arguments,
32 coroutine yields with signal-mask and floating-point rounding restoration,
return through uc_link, and process exit after returning with a null uc_link.
Its --layout-only mode inspects prepared contexts without restoring them.
environment-handoff checks same-runtime spawn environment transport.  Its
--exec, --spawn and --spawn-nowait modes take an inherited/mutated/large phase
and a child command; --inspect reports synthetic POSIX and Windows variables
separately.  Use it with an actual foreign-architecture MSYS interpreter and
a native Windows child to check the Windows-block fallback without changing
POSIX argv handling.  Matching architecture is a necessary condition for
the private-envp optimization, not proof of arbitrary runtime-version
compatibility.  Keep raw Windows exit codes separate from POSIX wait status.
winsup.api/signal-context-arm64 delivers a signal from another pthread while
q31 is live without intervening calls; it checks actual asynchronous floating
point context restoration and skips other architectures.
The ARM64-only signal-registers-arm64 target links its C driver with
signal-registers-arm64-asm.S and checks 16 asynchronous deliveries across
integer, full vector, stack/link and floating-point control state.
signal-myfault-arm64 checks eight protected-page uname calls return EFAULT.
It exercises the runtime's internal myfault personality, not the public
sigjmp signal handler.  Ordinary and debugger executions are separate
qualification surfaces; an ordinary pass does not prove debugger parity.
The uname-machine test checks the current uname import, the uname_x export
and the legacy uname export against the test executable's architecture.
An x86_64 executable must still report x86_64 under ARM64 emulation.
ctype-table checks the imported _ctype_ array's C-locale byte layout and stable
identity, separately from the locale-selected table pointer.  After installing
genuine hosted libstdc++ headers/libraries, build the optional
winsup.api/ctype-hosted target to exercise std::ctype<char>::classic_table(),
locale facets and iostream parsing/formatting against that same data export.
This optional target is not part of the freestanding runtime bootstrap.
winsup.api/runtime-process covers nested fork with heap and pipes, inherited
signals, byte-for-byte quoted arguments of lengths 1-40 across exec, signals
after exec, and pthread TLS/mutex isolation.  Its --direct-exec mode exits 55
only after the successor validates its arguments and handles its signal;
--native-exec FILE expects a native Windows fixture that exits 73.
winsup.api/process-group checks setpgid, WIFSTOPPED before WSTOPSIG, group
SIGCONT, and WIFEXITED before WEXITSTATUS.  These are process-group checks,
not a claim about interactive terminal job control.

Tests whose name is mentioned in XFAIL_TESTS are expected to fail, effectively
reversing the result of those.

Adding a test
=============

Add the source for the test under testsuite/winsup.api/.

Add the additional tests program to check_PROGRAMS in testsuite/Makefile.am.

(Note that if the test 'foo' has a single source file, foo.c, there's no need to
write a foo_SOURCES as that's the default Automake assumes.)

Tests can use libltp, but's that not required.

The Cygwin 'installation' that the tests are run in is minimal, so don't assume
anything is present.

Any test that needs to test command line args or redirection needs to run such a
child program itself, as the testsuite will not do any arguments or redirection
for it.  Same for fork, signals, etc.

Tests should either run successfully and exit(0), exit(77) to indicate a skipped
test, or any other exit status to indicate a failure.

Tips
====

* To run selected tests, use e.g:

$ make check TESTS="winsup.api/ltp/umask03 winsup.api/ltp/stat06"

* To build the tests without running them, use the check_programs target, e.g:

$ make -C winsup/testsuite/ check_programs

* To run an individual test program directly (against the installed, rather than
  just built DLL), e.g.:

$ winsup/testsuite/winsup.api/pthread/cancel1

* To run an individual test program against the test DLL under gdb, e.g.:

$ PATH="<build_tooldir>/winsup/testsuite/testinst/bin/:$PATH" cygrun -notimeout "gdb winsup.api/systemcall"
